John H. wrote:
> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

>> To satisfy the often repeated desire to further
>> differentiate the classes for the entire game, I
>> propose that "original equipment" have a special
>> status: it improves _slowly_ with age, but only
>> while it is be]ing carried by the adventurer.

> Hm, I'm not too fond of this idea.

Oh, well, the goal of pleasing everyone is illusary
at best.

> A big part of Nethack is the acquition and proper
> usage of items that better the player's state.

Yes. Sometimes to Monty Haul proportions, as with
the addition of Alchemy to the game leading to PCs
with 500 HPs, almost completely removing the need
for careful turn-by-turn play.

> Enchantment, erodeproofing, repair, resistances,
> cancellation and so on.

And a lot of the complaining here from more
experienced players is that the game loses a lot of
its luster exactly because that is possible to do.

> This would allow players to ignore much of that,

Well, no, issues of trading long term benefit for
short term gains would make the player think long
and hard about whether to take advanage of this
YANI's implementation, at all, and that added
richness is very much part of the intent of the
proposal.

> making for a very different game,

Compare and contrast with Artemis's note further
down the thread that NetHack should incorporate
"postive entropy" rather than this YANI's "negative
entropy for original kit", something I'd also
strongly endorse if only to make ascension kits much
harder to guarantee being possible of assembling.

> with the only requirement being survival.

That's always been the only requirement of NetHack.

Keeping this proposal game balanced while satisfying
the intended goal of more fully distinguishing the
roles is a challenge, of course, and likely it would
be got wrong a few times before being gotten right,
if the proposal were attempted at all.

Thanks for your comments, you've surely raised valid
issues.

